  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nA shortwave tracking southeast on northwest flow aloft over\nsouthern Montana and Wyoming will bring the next band of showers\nand isolated thunder. This will track out of Central Montana into\nthe Little Rockies, Petroleum, and Garfield Counties, mainly\nsouthwestern portions of the CWA for NE Montana. Modest CAPE/shear\nprofiles support embedded convection, though severe storms are not\nexpected. That said,do not be surprised to see locally heavy \ndownpours. This has further support from HREF showing 3 hour max \nQPF bulls-eyes ranging from 0.25-0.50\" across these areas tonight \nand Saturday at times. While heavy rainfall amounts should be \nisolated, this should be kept in mind.\n\nOtherwise, ensembles show upper ridging taking hold on Sunday and\nMonday for drier weather and warmer conditions. On Monday,the next\nshortwave looks to approach from the north with a weak cold front.\nCurrent support suggests northwest winds mixing down with this\nfeature, though conditions remaining mainly dry. Strong boundary\nlayer mixing, pre-frontal warming, afternoon humidity readings\nbelow 20 percent, and developing northwest wind gusts may raise\nfire weather concerns, something that may need to be considered.\nAdditional, will watch for potential for impacts to Fort Peck \nLake from the windy conditions which may raise wave heights and\nproduce difficult boating conditions.\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nConfidence is high on a band of showers and isolated thunder\nacross the southwest zones tonight and Saturday with excellent\nmodel consensus. NBM was sufficient here other than some minor\ntweaks to reflect near term trends and for consistency. That said,\nconfidence is below average on specific precipitation amounts\ngiven the nature of embedded convection. Ensembles are in good\nagreement overall on the ridge through Monday as well, raising\nconcerns for windy conditions and fire weather concerns as the\nupcoming focus.",
